ORBITAL-CORRIDORS(7)       TriadicFrameworks Manual       ORBITAL-CORRIDORS(7)
================================================================================
NAME
       orbital-corridors - cross-track corridors, phase corridors, and
                           resonance-safe orbital maneuvering for SAR fleets

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital corridors define:
           • cross-track safety bands
           • along-track and phase corridors
           • resonance-safe maneuver envelopes
           • constellation maneuver choreography
           • long-term orbital coherence

DESCRIPTION
       Orbital corridors describe the allowable geometric and resonance-time
       regions in which SAR platforms may maneuver without degrading
       constellation coherence, sweep alignment, or multi-pass fusion quality.

       Corridors ensure:
           • safe orbital adjustments
           • predictable revisit timing
           • stable resonance envelopes
           • harmonic-lock preservation
           • constellation-scale structural integrity

       WRSADC evaluates corridor boundaries before approving any maneuver.

CROSS-TRACK CORRIDORS
       Cross-track corridors define the lateral displacement limits between
       a platform’s ground track and the reference track used for SAR fusion.

       Variables:
           Δx_cross   cross-track offset
           Δθ_inc     incidence angle deviation
           ΔR_res     resonance envelope overlap

       Corridor types:

           narrow cross-track corridor
                  Strict lateral limits.
                  → tandem SAR, high-coherence missions.

           medium cross-track corridor
                  Moderate lateral drift allowed.
                  → multi-pass fusion, ladder patterns.

           wide cross-track corridor
                  Large lateral freedom.
                  → global coverage, swarm constellations.

           resonance cross-track corridor
                  Lateral drift allowed only if resonance envelopes remain
                  aligned across platforms.

       Exceeding cross-track corridors risks decorrelation and spectral drift.

PHASE CORRIDORS
       Phase corridors define allowable differences in orbital phase between
       platforms in a constellation.

       Variables:
           Δφ_orbit   orbital phase difference
           Δt_sweep   sweep timing offset
           Δf_orbit   orbital frequency mismatch

       Corridor types:

           strict phase corridor
                  Platforms must remain nearly phase-locked.
                  → ideal for interferometry and tandem SAR.

           harmonic phase corridor
                  Phase differences allowed only at harmonic intervals.
                  → supports harmonic-sync constellations.

           sliding phase corridor
                  Phase difference drifts slowly across cycles.
                  → supports adaptive sweep choreography.

           resonance phase corridor
                  Phase alignment required only when resonance windows open.

       Phase corridors determine when sweeps can be synchronized.

RESONANCE-SAFE ORBITAL MANEUVERING
       Resonance-safe maneuvering ensures that orbital adjustments do not
       disrupt resonance envelopes, harmonic-lock, or constellation coherence.

       Maneuver constraints:

           • maintain envelope overlap
           • preserve harmonic ratios
           • avoid gradient overload
           • remain within drift corridors
           • maintain lineage continuity

       Maneuver types:

           micro-adjustment
                  Small Δv corrections; safe within narrow corridors.

           corridor-shift maneuver
                  Moves platform from one corridor band to another.

           resonance-hold maneuver
                  Maintains harmonic-lock during drift.

           envelope-aligned maneuver
                  Adjusts orbit to maximize resonance envelope overlap.

           constellation maneuver
                  Multi-platform coordinated adjustment.

       WRSADC validates all maneuvers before execution.

TRIADIC MANEUVER CYCLES
       Maneuvers follow a triadic structure:

           P1 (begin)
               → maneuver intent declared
               → corridor boundaries evaluated

           P2 (transform)
               → Δv applied
               → resonance and spectral effects monitored

           P3 (close)
               → new orbit stabilized
               → lineage updated

       Nested maneuvers form ladder or hybrid orbital patterns.

CORRIDOR FAILURE MODES
       Corridor violations include:

           cross-track breach
                  Lateral drift exceeds corridor limits.

           phase corridor collapse
                  Orbital phase difference becomes incoherent.

           resonance envelope divergence
                  Platforms lose spectral alignment.

           harmonic desync
                  Orbital frequencies drift out of harmonic-lock.

           gradient overload
                  Maneuver induces excessive resonance gradient.

       Diagnostic signals:

           corridor-integrity score
           phase-alignment index
           resonance-overlap ratio
           harmonic-lock error
           drift-vector magnitude

       WRSADC halts unsafe maneuvers.

INTERACTION WITH SCHEMAS
       Orbital corridors require:

           • constellation-schema
           • resonance-schema for orbital sweeps
           • structural-schema for formation geometry
           • lineage-schema for multi-pass history

       Schemas ensure orbital behavior remains coherent.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:

           • corridor entry/exit events
           • maneuver history
           • resonance envelope evolution
           • phase drift trends
           • harmonic-lock continuity

       Lineage enables long-term orbital planning.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • computes corridor boundaries
           • validates maneuver safety
           • enforces coherence thresholds
           • choreographs constellation maneuvers
           • blocks unsafe orbital states

       WRSADC is the corridor guardian.

NOTES
       Orbital corridors extend RTT into orbital maneuvering. They transform
       SAR constellations into resonance-time–aware fleets capable of safe
       adjustments, coherent sweeps, and long-term orbital harmony across
       global sensing missions.

